  "scenario": "MPLS L3VPN BGP PE-CE Routing",
  "goals": "• {{router2_name}}, {{router3_name}} and {{router4_name}} form an MPLS backbone where OSPF area 0, the Loopback0 addresses and LDP are already configured and operational on the two core links {{link2_cidr}} and {{link3_cidr}}.\n    customer site A: {{router1_name}}, Loopback1 {{router1_loopback1_cidr}}, attached to {{router2_name}} over {{link1_cidr}}\n    MPLS backbone: {{router2_name}} (PE), {{router3_name}} (P), {{router4_name}} (PE)\n    customer site B: {{router5_name}}, Loopback1 {{router5_loopback1_cidr}}, attached to {{router4_name}} over {{link4_cidr}}\n• The VPN routing instance {{vrf_name}} already exists on {{router2_name}} and {{router4_name}} with route distinguisher {{rd}} and route target {{rt}}, the CE-facing interfaces are already inside it, and the VPNv4 iBGP session between the two PE Loopback0 addresses in AS {{as_number}} is already established.\n• Both customer routers already run BGP and already advertise their Loopback1 network, and both are already configured to peer with their PE:\n    {{router1_name}} is in AS {{ce1_as}} and peers with {{link1_ip2}}\n    {{router5_name}} is in AS {{ce2_as}} and peers with {{link4_ip1}}\n• Complete the PE side: inside the {{vrf_name}} IPv4 address family of AS {{as_number}}, peer with the local customer router over the VRF interface and activate that neighbor.\n• No redistribution of static or connected routes is allowed: the customer prefixes must enter the VPN through the PE-CE BGP session only.\n• Result: both PEs must hold both customer prefixes in their VPNv4 table, each customer router must learn the remote site prefix through BGP, and {{router1_name}} must reach {{router5_loopback1_cidr}}.",
